\section{The stationary solution}\label{sec:interior}

The stationary problem is solved in the three steps announced in \S\ref{sec:thesis}: factor the friction (\S\ref{sec:setup}), predict the signal (\S\ref{sec:meanrev}), and combine the two into a filter (\S\ref{sec:filter}). Throughout, fix a filtered probability space satisfying the usual conditions; Table~\ref{tab:notation} collects the notation. The return signal $\mu$ is adapted and mean-zero, with forecast curve
\[
\bar\mu(t,s) \;=\; \begin{cases}\mu_s, & s\le t,\\[2pt] \E_t[\mu_s], & s>t,\end{cases}
\]
decaying as $s\to\infty$, so that forecasts vanish at long horizons. The Fourier convention is $\hat f(\omega)=\int e^{i\omega t}f(t)\,dt$, under which causal kernels have transforms analytic in the upper half-plane. Admissible positions are adapted processes of finite friction energy $\E\langle x,Nx\rangle<\infty$; for $\eta=0$ this class admits positions with a Brownian component, whose trading rate carries white noise.

\subsection{Factorization of the friction}\label{sec:setup}

The symbol $\hat n$ of \eqref{eq:symbol} is positive and even, grows polynomially, and satisfies the Szeg\H{o} condition $\int|\log\hat n(\omega)|/(1+\omega^2)\,d\omega<\infty$, met by every kernel considered here, the pure power law included: its zero $\hat n\sim\gamma c_\beta|\omega|^{1+\beta}$ at $\omega=0$ is only logarithmic in $\log\hat n$, hence integrable \citep{Krein1962}. This log-integrability lets $N$ factor as $N=N_-N_+$ with symbol
\begin{equation}\label{eq:wh}
\hat n(\omega) = \hat n_-(\omega)\,\hat n_+(\omega), \qquad \hat n_- = \overline{\hat n_+},
\end{equation}
where $\hat n_+$ is analytic and zero-free in the upper half-plane. Then $N_+$ is causal with causal inverse, and $N_-=N_+^*$ is its anticausal adjoint. Conjugation by such a factor whitens the friction: under it the inner product $\langle\,\cdot\,,N\,\cdot\,\rangle$ becomes the flat $L^2$ product. The rate-referred factors carry one time-integration on each side, $\hat q_\pm=\hat n_\pm/(\mp i\omega)$. The outer factor $\hat n_+$ is given by the Szeg\H{o} formula
\begin{equation}\label{eq:szego}
\hat n_+(\omega) \;=\; \exp\!\Bigl(\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\Bigl(\frac{1}{t-\omega}-\frac{t}{1+t^2}\Bigr)\log \hat n(t)\,dt\Bigr), \qquad \operatorname{Im}\omega>0,
\end{equation}
unique up to a unimodular constant \citep{Krein1962}. Because $\hat n$ is even, evaluating \eqref{eq:szego} on the imaginary axis reduces to the real Poisson integral $\Phi(\theta):=\hat n_+(i\theta)$ of \eqref{eq:phi}, used repeatedly below.

\begin{assumption}[Friction]\label{ass:friction}
The weights satisfy $\eta,\gamma,\lambda\ge0$; the propagator $g$ is locally integrable and positive-definite, $\hat g\ge0$ in \eqref{eq:symbol}; and the symbol $\hat n$ is positive almost everywhere and satisfies the Szeg\H{o} condition, so that the factorization \eqref{eq:wh} exists.
\end{assumption}

\subsection{Prediction of the signal}\label{sec:meanrev}

The adapted trade responds to the signal's expected future. The trader cannot see the future path of $\mu$ and replaces it by the forecast curve $\bar\mu(t,s)=\E_t[\mu_s]$, $s\ge t$. For a Gaussian signal the forecast is a linear functional of the past; it reduces to a linear operator on the signal itself, or on a finite Markov lift, when the signal is finite-dimensional.

\begin{definition}[Markov signal]\label{def:mr}
A stationary signal is Markov of order $d$ if its forecast curve is a deterministic combination of the current value and its first $d-1$ derivatives,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:collapse}
\bar\mu(t,s) \;=\; \sum_{k=0}^{d-1}\mu^{(k)}_t\,g_k(s-t)\qquad (s\ge t),\qquad g_k(0)=\delta_{k0},
\end{equation}
equivalently if its spectral density is rational of degree $d$.
\end{definition}

Such a signal is a linear readout $\mu_t=c^{\!\top}X_t$ of a $d$-dimensional Markov state --- its lift; in observable coordinates the lift is $(\mu_t,\dot\mu_t,\dots,\mu^{(d-1)}_t)$, and \eqref{eq:collapse} carries the whole future in finitely many numbers. Consistency pins the profiles down: the tower property $\E_t[\bar\mu(t',s)]=\bar\mu(t,s)$ forces the mean dynamics to be linear, so the $g_k$ solve a $d$th-order constant-coefficient ODE --- exponential modes $e^{-r_j(s-t)}$, or damped oscillations when the rates $r_j$ are complex.

The mean-reverting case is $d=1$: the forecast is the current value times one profile, $\bar\mu(t,s)=\mu_t f(s-t)$, and the tower property forces $f(\tau)=e^{-\theta\tau}$ with $\theta>0$ the mean-reversion rate. This is the Ornstein--Uhlenbeck signal --- the only stationary Gaussian signal with a fixed forecast profile, by Doob's theorem --- and it also covers the non-Markov, non-Gaussian moving averages $\mu_t=\int_{-\infty}^t e^{-\theta(t-r)}\sigma_r\,dW_r$ with adapted stochastic volatility $\sigma$. For $d=2$ the forecast combines the signal and its velocity, a damped oscillation when the two rates are complex.

A signal with no finite-dimensional state has no such reduction: its forecast is the Wiener--Kolmogorov predictor, a linear operator on the whole past. Take $\mu$ stationary, Gaussian, purely non-deterministic, and generating its own filtration $\F_t=\sigma(\mu_s:s\le t)$. Its spectral density factors as $S_\mu=|\hat\psi|^2$ with $\hat\psi$ the outer (causal, zero-free) spectral factor, so $\mu=\psi\ast\dot W$ is a causal moving average of its innovations $\dot W$, the unit white noise generating the filtration --- the Wold representation \citep{Wiener1949,Whittle1963}. The forecast keeps the innovations up to $t$ and drops the later ones.

\subsection{The trading filter}\label{sec:filter}

The factorization and the forecast combine through the adapted inverse of $N$. The first-order condition \eqref{eq:foc} inverts the projected operator $P_+NP_+$, which the one-sided factors assemble. The lemma is stated for a general factored positive operator; it is applied to $N$ here, to the rate-referred $Q$ in Section~\ref{sec:fractional}, and to the finite-horizon operator in Section~\ref{sec:boundary}.

\begin{lemma}[Adapted projected inverse]\label{lem:pi}
Let $A=A_-A_+$ be a positive operator with $A_+$ causal with causal inverse and $A_-=A_+^*$ anticausal with anticausal inverse. Then, on adapted processes,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:pi}
(P_+\,A\,P_+)^{-1} \;=\; A_+^{-1}\,P_+\,A_-^{-1}.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

The proof, in Appendix~\ref{app:A}, is triangular bookkeeping: causality makes $A_+$ and its inverse preserve the adapted subspace, adjointness makes $A_-$ and its inverse preserve the orthogonal complement, and the projection annihilates what lands there. Among all square roots of $\hat n$, the factorization \eqref{eq:wh} selects the one whose whitening $N_-^{-1}$ leaves the space of processes invisible to adapted strategies invariant; this is the sense in which it is Cholesky with respect to the filtration.

\begin{remark}[Relation to operator factorization]\label{rmk:nest}
Lemma~\ref{lem:pi} is the projected-inverse identity behind the triangular factorization of a positive operator relative to a nest of subspaces \citep{GohbergKrein1970,Arveson1975}. Here the nest is the increasing family of adapted subspaces indexed by the filtration, and \eqref{eq:wh} is the corresponding filtration-indexed Cholesky factorization: the scalar Szeg\H{o} outer factor in the stationary case, and the Gohberg--Krein Volterra factor on a finite horizon (Section~\ref{sec:boundary}).
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Optimal policy, general adapted signal]\label{thm:general}
Under Assumption~\ref{ass:friction}, the unique adapted optimum of \eqref{eq:objective} is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:policy}
x^\star \;=\; N_+^{-1}\,P_+\,N_-^{-1}\,\mu,
\qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad
x^\star_t = \bigl(N_+^{-1}\zeta\bigr)(t), \quad
\zeta_s = \bigl(N_-^{-1}\,\bar\mu(s,\cdot)\bigr)(s),
\end{equation}
where the second form uses the commutation of the deterministic operator $N_-^{-1}$ with conditional expectation on its argument.
\end{theorem}

The three factors in \eqref{eq:policy} are the three steps of the classical recipe: an anticausal whitening, the adapted projection, and a causal recolouring. The whitening would call for the future of the signal; the projection supplies instead its forecast (\S\ref{sec:meanrev}). Only the friction's factor whitens the signal, the signal's own spectral factor entering through the projection alone. Completing the square gives a second reading. Up to a constant the objective \eqref{eq:objective} equals $-\tfrac12\|x-N^{-1}\mu\|_N^2$ in the friction norm $\|y\|_N^2=\E\langle y,Ny\rangle$, so that $x^\star$ is the orthogonal projection, in that norm, of the anticipative optimum $N^{-1}\mu$ onto the adapted positions --- equivalently the causal Wiener--Kolmogorov estimate of the whitened return from the past.

\begin{remark}[Relation to Wiener--Kolmogorov prediction]\label{rmk:wk}
The construction is the Wiener--Hopf solution of a Wiener--Kolmogorov estimation problem, with the friction in place of the signal covariance. Linear prediction whitens a stationary signal by its own spectral factor $\hat\psi$, keeps the causal part, and recolours, returning the least-squares forecast from the past \citep{Wiener1949,Whittle1963}. The same three steps, carried out with the friction's factor $\hat n_+$ in place of $\hat\psi$, give the optimal position: by the estimation reading above, $x^\star$ is the causal estimate of the anticipative optimum $N^{-1}\mu$ in the friction metric. Prediction and trading are thus one construction on two quadratic forms, the signal covariance and the friction --- the correspondence that carries frequency-domain factorization from filtering into linear--quadratic control \citep{Whiteman1985}.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[The nonanticipativity multiplier]\label{rmk:duality}
Substituting \eqref{eq:policy} into $Nx^\star$ writes the first-order condition \eqref{eq:foc} without the projection,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:multiplier}
N\,x^\star = \mu - \xi^\star, \qquad \xi^\star = N_-\,(I-P_+)\,N_-^{-1}\mu, \qquad P_+\xi^\star = 0,
\end{equation}
so the adapted optimum solves the unconstrained first-order condition for the modified return $\mu-\xi^\star$. A multiplier with vanishing optional projection is the defining property of the nonanticipativity multipliers of \citet{RockafellarWets1976}, established there for general convex multistage problems; \eqref{eq:multiplier} is their closed form here. Because $\E\langle x,\xi^\star\rangle=0$ for adapted $x$, the value forgone to adaptedness equals the friction energy $\tfrac12\E\langle\xi^\star,N^{-1}\xi^\star\rangle$ of the multiplier, per unit time in the stationary case --- the shadow price of information of \S\ref{sec:value} below.
\end{remark}

\begin{assumption}[Signal]\label{ass:signal}
The return signal $\mu$ is stationary, Gaussian, purely non-deterministic, and generates its own filtration, with Wold representation $\mu=\psi\ast\dot W$ and spectral density $S_\mu=|\hat\psi|^2$, $\hat\psi$ the outer spectral factor.
\end{assumption}

For a stationary signal the projection is itself a filter. In the innovations $\dot W$ the whitened return has symbol $h:=\hat\psi\,\hat n_-^{-1}$; since conditioning at time $s$ discards the innovations after $s$, the projection truncates the moving-average kernel at lag zero and acts on symbols as the Riesz plus-part $\pluspart{\,\cdot\,}$ onto non-negative lags. Then \eqref{eq:policy} is a transfer function.

\begin{theorem}[Optimal trading filter]\label{thm:filter}
Under Assumptions~\ref{ass:friction} and~\ref{ass:signal}, and $\lambda>0$, the optimal position is the linear filter $x^\star=\pi\ast\dot W$ with transfer function
\begin{equation}\label{eq:filter}
\hat\pi \;=\; \hat n_+^{-1}\,\pluspart{\,h\,},
\qquad h = \hat\psi\,\hat n_-^{-1},
\end{equation}
the optimal rate the filter $\hat\chi=(-i\omega)\,\hat\pi$, and the attained value rate, the expected profit per unit time, is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:value}
v \;=\; \frac{1}{4\pi}\,\bigl\|\pluspart{h}\bigr\|_{L^2}^2 .
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

For a Markov signal the same filter reads on the observed signal rather than the innovations. The whitened forecast then reduces to a differential operator: with $\rho_k=(N_-^{-1}g_k)(0)$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:zeta-collapse}
\zeta_s = (N_-^{-1}\bar\mu(s,\cdot))(s) = \sum_{k=0}^{d-1}\rho_k\,\mu^{(k)}_s = P(\partial)\,\mu_s,
\end{equation}
so that the policy filters the signal and its derivatives, $\hat x^\star(\omega)=P(-i\omega)\,\hat\mu(\omega)/\hat n_+(\omega)$, a rational filter. For the Ornstein--Uhlenbeck signal ($d=1$) the single pole at $\omega=-i\theta$ gives $P(\partial)=\rho=1/\hat n_-(-i\theta)=1/\Phi(\theta)$ (Appendix~\ref{app:B}), with
\begin{equation}\label{eq:phi}
\Phi(\theta) \;:=\; \hat n_+(i\theta) \;=\; \exp\Bigl[\frac{\theta}{2\pi}\int\frac{\log\hat n(t)}{\theta^2+t^2}\,dt\Bigr] \;>\;0,
\end{equation}
the outer factor of $\hat n$ on the imaginary axis, so the position filter is the single formula covering all three frictions,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ou-filter}
\hat x^\star(\omega) \;=\; H(\omega)\,\hat\mu(\omega), \qquad H(\omega)=\frac{1}{\Phi(\theta)\,\hat n_+(\omega)},
\end{equation}
with value $v=\sigma^2\theta/4\Phi(\theta)^2$, where $\theta^2\sigma^2$ is the innovation variance of $\mu$ ($\sigma^2$ that of $\alpha=\mu/\theta$). For $d=2$ the filter uses the signal and its velocity.

The hypothesis $\lambda>0$ keeps the whitened return and the position filter in $L^2$: without risk aversion $\hat n$ vanishes at $\omega=0$ and the position is non-stationary --- the regime of Section~\ref{sec:fractional}, where the theory is referred to the rate. The rate filter $\hat\chi$ lies in $L^2$ under the spectral-decay hypothesis of Appendix~\ref{app:B}, and otherwise, notably for the exponential kernel without temporary cost, carries a lag-zero white-noise atom (Section~\ref{sec:surfing}).

\subsection{The value of information}\label{sec:value}

The value \eqref{eq:value} has a benchmark. The perfectly-anticipating trader replaces the projection $\pluspart{\,\cdot\,}$ by the identity, extracting
\begin{equation}\label{eq:vant}
v_{\rm ant} \;=\; \frac{1}{4\pi}\int \frac{S_\mu}{\hat n}\,d\omega,
\end{equation}
the value attainable with the whole signal path in hand. The \emph{causality gap} is the ratio $v/v_{\rm ant}\le1$, the fraction of the clairvoyant value that survives the restriction to current information. The shortfall $v_{\rm ant}-v$ equals, by \eqref{eq:value}, the $L^2$ energy of the anticausal part of the half-whitened signal $h=\hat\psi\hat n_-^{-1}$, and by Remark~\ref{rmk:duality} the friction energy of the nonanticipativity multiplier $\xi^\star$ --- the shadow price of adaptedness, in closed form. For an Ornstein--Uhlenbeck signal the value $v=\sigma^2\theta/4\Phi(\theta)^2$ turns on the friction factor $\Phi(\theta)=\hat n_+(i\theta)$: at fixed appreciation variance a faster signal carries a proportionally larger expected return, $\mu=\theta\alpha$, and how the impact tempers the resulting growth separates the frictions (Figure~\ref{fig:value}a). Pure risk and finite-memory exponential impact have bounded factors, $\Phi\to\sqrt\lambda$ and $\Phi\to\sqrt{2\kappa\gamma+\lambda}$, so the value keeps the frictionless growth $v\propto\theta^2$; a fast signal is then fully valuable, and under the exponential kernel it is traded by riding its own decaying impact residual, the impact surfing of \S\ref{sec:surfing}. A long-memory power law has an unbounded factor, $\Phi\propto\theta^{(1+\beta)/2}$, so the value grows only sub-linearly, $v\propto\theta^{1-\beta}$. A temporary cost gives $\Phi\propto\sqrt\eta\,\theta$ at high speed, and the value saturates at $\mathrm{Var}(\alpha)/2\eta$ for $\theta\gtrsim\sqrt{\lambda/\eta}$: the cost of trading fast makes a fast signal worth no more than a slow one. The rule of thumb that fast signals are too expensive to trade is thus the regime of temporary cost or long-memory impact, not of the model as a whole. Under pure power-law impact the causality gap is scale-free, independent of the signal speed (\S\ref{sec:fracpolicy}):
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sinlaw}
\frac{v}{v_{\rm ant}} \;=\; \sin\!\frac{\pi\beta}{2}.
\end{equation}
Adaptedness is nearly free for short-memory impact ($\beta\to1$) and forfeits almost all the value for near-permanent impact ($\beta\to0$); at the empirical $\beta\in(0.2,0.6)$ the adapted trader keeps between a third and four-fifths of the clairvoyant value (Figure~\ref{fig:value}b).

