281:the value attainable with the whole signal path in hand. The \emph{causality gap} is the ratio $v/v_{\rm ant}\le1$, the fraction of the clairvoyant value that survives the restriction to current information. The shortfall $v_{\rm ant}-v$ equals, by \eqref{eq:value}, the $L^2$ energy of the anticausal part of the half-whitened signal $h=\hat\psi\hat n_-^{-1}$, and by Remark~\ref{rmk:duality} the friction energy of the nonanticipativity multiplier $\xi^\star$ --- the shadow price of adaptedness, in closed form. For an Ornstein--Uhlenbeck signal the value $v=\sigma^2\theta/4\Phi(\theta)^2$ turns on the friction factor $\Phi(\theta)=\hat n_+(i\theta)$: at fixed appreciation variance a faster signal carries a proportionally larger expected return, $\mu=\theta\alpha$, and how the impact tempers the resulting growth separates the frictions (Figure~\ref{fig:value}a). Pure risk and finite-memory exponential impact have bounded factors, $\Phi\to\sqrt\lambda$ and $\Phi\to\sqrt{2\kappa\gamma+\lambda}$, so the value keeps the frictionless growth $v\propto\theta^2$; a fast signal is then fully valuable, and under the exponential kernel it is traded by riding its own decaying impact residual, the impact surfing of \S\ref{sec:surfing}. A long-memory power law has an unbounded factor, $\Phi\propto\theta^{(1+\beta)/2}$, so the value grows only sub-linearly, $v\propto\theta^{1-\beta}$. A temporary cost gives $\Phi\propto\sqrt\eta\,\theta$ at high speed, and the value saturates at $\mathrm{Var}(\alpha)/2\eta$ for $\theta\gtrsim\sqrt{\lambda/\eta}$: the cost of trading fast makes a fast signal worth no more than a slow one. The rule of thumb that fast signals are too expensive to trade is thus the regime of temporary cost or long-memory impact, not of the model as a whole. Under pure power-law impact the causality gap is scale-free, independent of the signal speed (\S\ref{sec:fracpolicy}):
299:With $\eta=\lambda=0$ the friction symbol $\hat n=\gamma c_\beta|\omega|^{1+\beta}$ vanishes at $\omega=0$: the optimal position is non-stationary, and the stationary object is the rate. This section therefore works in the rate variable and the appreciation signal $\alpha_t=\E_t\int_t^\infty\mu_s\,ds$ of \S\ref{sec:problem}, with forecast curve $\bar\alpha(t,s)=\E_t[\alpha_s]=\int_s^\infty\bar\mu(t,r)\,dr$; for the Ornstein--Uhlenbeck signal $\alpha=\mu/\theta$. Referred to the rate the friction is $Q$ with symbol $\hat q=\hat n/\omega^2=\gamma\hat g$ and factors $\hat q_\pm=\hat n_\pm/(\mp i\omega)$; Lemma~\ref{lem:pi} applies to $Q=Q_-Q_+$ as it does to $N$, and the policy of Theorem~\ref{thm:general} reads
317:whose increment regularizes the weight $s^{-1-\nu}$ and keeps the integral finite for bounded, non-decaying $f$. The power-law symbol is a fractional power of frequency: for $g(t)=|t|^{-\beta}$ the transform is $\hat g(\omega)=c_\beta|\omega|^{\beta-1}$, so the Wiener--Hopf factors are fractional integrals, $Q_\pm=(\gamma c_\beta)^{1/2}I^\nu_\pm$ with $\nu=(1-\beta)/2$, and their inverses are the fractional derivatives $D^\nu_\pm$. The rate policy \eqref{eq:rate-policy} then reads
422:The portfolio and execution rules of the literature are special cases of the factorization, in two groups. When the friction is stationary and rational --- pure risk, temporary cost, exponential resilience, and their combinations --- the Szeg\H{o} factor is a ratio of polynomials, and the optimal position is a finite sum of exponential moving averages of the signal (Section~\ref{sec:filter}); Markowitz, the G\^arleanu--Pedersen aim portfolio, and the stationary Neuman--Vo\ss{} solution are the cases with zero, one, and two averages. When the horizon is finite, the Gohberg--Krein factor of Section~\ref{sec:finiteT} takes over, producing the liquidation profiles of the execution literature --- the power-law U-shape and Fredholm solutions, and the exponential block-plus-continuous strategy of Obizhaeva--Wang. Beyond these two, a general propagator has no closed factor; \S\ref{sec:general} identifies the factorization with the general-propagator solutions of the literature. Figure~\ref{fig:structure} shows how the memory of the impact sets the shape of the policy across this family.
